                <pb facs="0650" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/5464: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Irkutsk</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harris</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >temporarily at Omsk</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-25T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 25,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your 437, October 23, 6 p.m. The Department has taken up urgently with Stevens
                    the question of the withdrawal of the Allied railway inspectors. The last
                    message from Stevens indicates that it may not be necessary to withdraw them
                    immediately upon the departure of the Czechs. It remains, however, of the utmost
                    importance that the Russian railway officials assume a conciliatory and
                    compliant attitude.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
